<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Manetho</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">fl. early 3rd century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Manetho was an Egyptian priest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sebennytos">Sebennytos</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile-delta">Nile Delta</a> who wrote a history of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> in Greek, the Aegyptiaca, under the early Ptolemies in the 3rd century BCE. His division of Egyptian kings into thirty dynasties is still the basis of Egyptian chronology. The work itself is lost and survives only in excerpts and king lists quoted by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josephus">Josephus</a> and by the Christian chroniclers Africanus and Eusebius.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/manetho.jpg" alt="Manetho" width="320" height="153" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Waddell, W. G. (William Gillan), 1884-1945 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Manetho_Waddell1940_PD_Excerpt_pIX.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Little is certain about the life of Manetho. His name is a Greek form of an Egyptian name, and scholars have no agreement on the original: it may invoke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thoth">Thoth</a> or Neith, or be an occupational name meaning shepherd or groom. He came from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sebennytos">Sebennytos</a> and was probably a priest of the sun god Ra at Heliopolis, and an authority on the temple cult of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serapis">Serapis</a>.</p><p>He worked at the beginning of the Hellenistic order in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ptolemy-i-soter">Ptolemy I Soter</a> founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ptolemaic-kingdom">Ptolemaic Kingdom</a> in 305 BCE. Manetho wrote under Ptolemy I or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ptolemy-ii-philadelphus">Ptolemy II Philadelphus</a> to preserve the history of his homeland and, since he wrote in Greek, for its new foreign rulers. Ancient writers grouped him with the Mesopotamian writer Berossus, and both used king-lists as the framework of their histories.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Eight works have been attributed to Manetho, but modern scholars agree only that he wrote the Aegyptiaca. This was a chronological history in three scrolls, perhaps written in answer to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a>. It ran from a mythical age of divine rulers through the unification of Upper and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-egypt">Lower Egypt</a> by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/menes">Menes</a> and thirty or thirty-one dynasties to the establishment of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ptolemaic-kingdom">Ptolemaic Kingdom</a> in 305 BCE, and treated periods of stability against internal strife and foreign rule by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyksos">Hyksos</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kushite-empire">Kushites</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenids</a>.</p><p>Manetho coined the word dynasty, but he meant groups of rulers separated by a break, of place or of descent, not simply a line of blood relatives. His division of the rulers into thirty or thirty-one dynasties remains the chronological framework of Egyptology. Because he wrote in Greek, his history stayed readable after the last hieroglyphic text of 394 CE. His own writings are lost, and the fragments survive through later authors including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josephus">Josephus</a>, Africanus, Eusebius and Syncellus.</p><p>In the account preserved by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josephus">Josephus</a>, Manetho described the departure of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/israelites">Israelites</a> as the expulsion of a colony of lepers and ritually polluted people, led by the renegade priest Osarseph whom he identified with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moses">Moses</a>. Scholars read this as an answer to the anti-Egyptian side of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-exodus">the Exodus</a> story.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Manetho” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>A priest could serve the new kings, as <b>Manetho</b> did when he was commissioned to write a history of Egypt, or lead a revolt against them, as Horwennefer did in 205 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/societies/ptolemaic-egypt">Social Hierarchy of Ptolemaic Egypt</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Manetho</b> counts the Persian kings as the Twenty-seventh Dynasty.</blockquote><a href="/state/achaemenid-egypt">Achaemenid Egypt</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Manetho's</b> Seventh Dynasty, of 'seventy kings in seventy days', is now usually regarded as fictitious, and its kings are counted with the Eighth.</blockquote><a href="/state/eighth-dynasty-of-egypt">Eighth Dynasty of Egypt</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Manetho</b> placed the dynasty at Xois in the western Delta, but most scholars now locate it at Avaris.</blockquote><a href="/state/fourteenth-dynasty-of-egypt">Fourteenth Dynasty of Egypt</a></li><li><blockquote>Egypt again became a satrapy of the Achaemenid Empire, and <b>Manetho</b> counts the Persian kings as the Thirty-first Dynasty.</blockquote><a href="/state/thirty-first-dynasty-of-egypt">Thirty-first Dynasty of Egypt</a></li><li><blockquote>Around 720 BCE the Kushite king Shabaka conquered the Delta; <b>Manetho</b> says he burned Bakenranef alive.</blockquote><a href="/state/twenty-fourth-dynasty-of-egypt">Twenty-fourth Dynasty of Egypt</a></li></ul></section>
